{"name":"Basswood Green","hex":"#839e83","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basswood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several trees of the genus Tilia; the lindens, especially Tilia americana, the American basswood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basswood"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tilia americana","blocks":[{"text":"Tilia americana is a species of tree in the family Malvaceae. It is native to eastern North America, from southeast Manitoba east to New Brunswick, southwest to northeast Oklahoma, southeast to South Carolina, and west along the Niobrara River to Cherry County, Nebraska. It is the sole representative of its genus in the Western Hemisphere, assuming T. caroliniana is treated as a subspecies or local ecotype of T. americana. Common names include American basswood and American linden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Tilia_americana_%28American_Linden%29_%2828268263222%29.jpg/330px-Tilia_americana_%28American_Linden%29_%2828268263222%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tilia_americana","wikidata":"Q163981"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG/500px-Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG/1280px-Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG/500px-Tilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A specimen in the Arnold Arboretum leafing out in spring","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATilia_americana%2C_Arnold_Arboretum_-_IMG_5911.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Tilia_americana_sections.jpg/500px-Tilia_americana_sections.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Tilia_ame